In this lesson we'll look at back-to-back stem-and-leaf plots, a handy way to compare two data sets side by side without losing any of the original values. So here's how it works. The stem sits right in the middle, and it represents the tens or hundreds digit of your numbers. Then you get leaves on both sides. The leaves on one side belong to your first data set, and you read them starting from the stem and moving outward, so right to left. The leaves on the other side belong to your second data set, and you read those the normal way, left to right. Each leaf is just a single digit, one observation per leaf. The great thing about this layout is that you can glance at both sides and immediately see the shape, the centre, and the spread of each data set, all at once. It's perfect for spotting whether one group tends to score higher or is more spread out than the other. Now let's see it in action.